# Line endings. Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true by default, so a
# fresh Windows clone gets CRLF in every tracked file. Nothing here is consumed by
# a Windows-native tool, but plenty is consumed by bash and by Linux containers,
# where a trailing \r is not whitespace — it is part of the word:
#
#   scripts/dev.sh: line 28: set: pipefail^M: invalid option name
#
# Pinned per-extension rather than with a blanket `* text=auto`: a blanket rule
# would also renormalize files that are legitimately CRLF in the index today
# (apps/web/shared/*.ts, docs/external/vndb/api.md), turning the next touch of
# each into a whole-file diff.
*.sh          text eol=lf
*.bash        text eol=lf
*.go          text eol=lf
*.yml         text eol=lf
*.yaml        text eol=lf
*.env         text eol=lf
*.env.example text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile  text eol=lf
Dockerfile    text eol=lf
Caddyfile     text eol=lf

# Test fixtures are bytes under test, never normalized. The bangumi wiki spec
# corpus is CRLF on purpose — its cases are about line structure.
**/testdata/** -text

*.dump binary
*.webp binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.avif binary
